WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Do you have
multiple Kubernetes clusters

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:04.800
across different clouds
in your own data centers?

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.650
Then you know how challenging
it is to manage them.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.140
How can you simplify your
multi-cluster strategy

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.130
with Anthos?

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.510
Keep watching to find out.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.772
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.970
Hi, I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and welcome to this episode

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:24.660
of Anthos 101 where we will
cover the reasons for adopting

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.620
a multi-cluster strategy and
how Anthos can help you manage

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.320
multiple clusters easily.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.960
Why would you adopt a
multi-cluster strategy?

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.360
Well, the answer is
one of two reasons.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.830
Your workload needs isolation,
or you need compute anywhere.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.020
Let's dive deeper
into isolation.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.190
By isolation, we often mean
both isolation of control plane

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.740
and the isolation of data plane.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.800
The most obvious reason to
leverage multiple clusters

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.190
is to separate your prod and
non-prod environments mainly

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.020
to ensure the production
environment is

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.270
tightly controlled
and not compromised

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.700
during development and testing.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.790
The second reason is
controlling the blast radius.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.850
You may be running the same
app across multiple clusters

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.010
just to ensure that
the application remains

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.530
resilient to failures
or changes occurring

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.400
in the single cluster.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.910
The other reason could
be tenant separation.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.010
You would do this for
a variety of reasons,

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.500
like, you've identified
some applications that

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.260
don't play well together and
want to keep them separated.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.010
You're running a mix of
trusted and untrusted code

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.010
and want stronger
boundaries between them.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.020
You've got some really
important services

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.720
that you want to
ensure are always up.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.630
And finally, you may want
to isolate for security

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.700
and compliance reasons.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.860
Now, the other
dimension to consider

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:46.020
when adopting a
multi-cluster strategy

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.110
is the need to provide
compute platform anywhere

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.320
and everywhere your organization
deploys applications.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.930
You might want to co-locate
your Kubernetes instances

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.960
with existing infrastructure
allowing you to modernize

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.050
applications that sit directly
near those hard-to-move

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.610
databases and stable services.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.730
Availability could be
another driving factor.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.910
Running applications across
regions and even clouds

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.160
can help ensure your
application remains up

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.740
and running despite any outages.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.110
Regulatory requirements
also lend themselves

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.410
to multiple clusters, especially
for multinationals with data

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.030
residency requirements.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.660
And we know that each cloud
has differentiated services

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.850
that development
teams want to use

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.300
making developer choice
another important factor.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.130
Now that we know
why you may need

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.600
to adopt a
multi-cluster strategy,

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.780
let's talk about the
operational challenges

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.800
of maintaining multiple clusters
across clouds and your own data

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.350
centers.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.660
In most organizations,
the platform teams

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.210
are responsible for
developing custom solutions

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.270
or cobbling together open-source
tooling for practically

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:51.110
everything.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:52.850
And this leads to
a lot of friction

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.250
in adopting a
multi-cluster strategy.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.550
Managing the lifecycle of each
cluster using different tooling

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:02.090
from different vendors, ensuring
uniform access and governance

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.100
controls across the cluster--

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.070
getting insights into your
global set of applications,

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:10.820
enabling application teams to
efficiently deliver software

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.550
to all their clusters
and managing traffic

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.770
to provide redundancy
and to reduce latency.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.975
This is what we set out
to solve with Anthos.

00:03:19.975 --> 00:03:22.430
Let's cover a few of the
multi-cluster solutions

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.140
we have in Anthos.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.190
Anthos GKE gives your team a
consistent container management

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:29.360
experience.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.540
Anthos Config Management
unifies access policies

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.840
and provides governance
across multiple clusters.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.690
Anthos Service Mesh allows
you to set and monitor

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:42.590
SLOs on your services, secure
traffic between services

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:44.570
even across the
cluster boundary.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:46.610
And ingress for
Anthos allows you

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.340
to load-balance external
traffic to the closest

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.830
cluster to your end users.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:55.190
This helps you to build low
latency and high availability

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:58.310
services in multiple
GCP regions.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.110
To make it easier
to take advantage

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.450
of these multi-cluster
features, we

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.020
are introducing a
concept called environs.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.930
With environs, you can
group related resources,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:12.200
like clusters, into a
single management domain.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.510
Once resources are grouped
together in an environ,

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.560
you can then easily manage
multi-cluster features

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.800
and components across
all environ members.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.110
This simple, yet,
powerful concept

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.290
allows you to up-level your
management from cluster

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.530
by cluster to group of clusters.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:34.310
For example, with environs,
you can now apply and enforce

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:37.880
common policy and config
across all environ members

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.280
using Anthos Config Management.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.980
Create a service
mesh over all members

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:48.290
of an environ using Anthos
Service Mesh and even

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.890
load-balance external traffic
into your GKE clusters

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.230
using ingress for Anthos.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:55.280
There you have it.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.850
Anthos simplifies the adoption
of multi-cluster strategy

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:03.230
across clouds and on-prem
with the concept of environs.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.540
Going forward, all
multi-cluster features

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.980
will be managed with environs.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.510
So expect more in this space.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:12.200
Check out the Environs Best
Practices Guide linked below

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.730
and get started today.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:15.530
For more Anthos
content, don't forget

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:18.950
to subscribe to follow the
entire Anthos 101 playlist.

